WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.557
Picture a café kitchen on a Saturday morning.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:11.588
Order slips hang on six rails, and each rail belongs to one cook, who works its slips in order.

3
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:15.118
Orders pour in, so the owner calls in four more cooks.

4
00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:20.320
They stand by the door with nothing to do, because every rail already has its cook.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.887
Paid to admire the rails.

6
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.037
That's a Kafka consumer group.

7
00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:31.588
Kafka keeps a log of records, split into partitions, and in a consumer group each partition has exactly one reader.

8
00:00:31.588 --> 00:00:36.316
The texting program reads six partitions, so a seventh reader sits idle.

9
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.861
Six readers send 30 texts a second.

10
00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:41.762
On a sale day, payments reach 300 a second.

11
00:00:41.762 --> 00:00:45.608
Wouldn't it be nice if any free cook could take any slip?

12
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.305
Kafka 4.2 can do that.

13
00:00:47.305 --> 00:00:49.083
It's called a share group.

14
00:00:49.083 --> 00:00:51.339
Its readers don't own partitions.

15
00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:58.520
They all read from all of them, and the broker, the server that runs Kafka, hands out records one by one.

16
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.326
But then, what stops two cooks from making the same order?

17
00:01:02.326 --> 00:01:03.513
Hold that question.

18
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:12.385
Let's shrink it to one partition with eight text messages, and three readers, c1, c2 and c3, in the share group notify.

19
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.127
The broker keeps a state for every record.

20
00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:18.297
Each one starts out available, waiting for a reader.

21
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.675
And it keeps a start offset: every record before it is finished.

22
00:01:22.675 --> 00:01:23.914
Right now that's 0.

23
00:01:23.914 --> 00:01:25.219
Nothing is finished.

24
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.696
At ten o'clock, each reader asks for two records.

25
00:01:28.696 --> 00:01:33.204
Each one it gets is acquired: locked to that reader for 30 seconds.

26
00:01:33.204 --> 00:01:35.895
And its delivery count goes from 0 to 1.

27
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:40.752
In the kitchen, a cook who takes a slip has 30 seconds to say how it went.

28
00:01:40.752 --> 00:01:45.789
That's the answer to our question: a locked slip can't be taken by anyone else.

29
00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:47.765
Records 6 and 7 stay available.

30
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:50.431
Each reader answers for each record.

31
00:01:50.431 --> 00:01:51.679
ACCEPT means done.

32
00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:55.079
c2's texts went out, so 2 and 3 are acknowledged.

33
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:55.968
c3 sent 4.

34
00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:59.826
But the texting company timed out on 5, which may pass.

35
00:01:59.826 --> 00:02:04.245
RELEASE gives it back: available again, with its count still 1.

36
00:02:04.245 --> 00:02:06.771
REJECT would give up on it for good.

37
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:11.667
The start offset stays at 0, because records 0 and 1 aren't finished.

38
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.579
Now c2 asks again, for two records.

39
00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:16.263
Which two does it get?

40
00:02:16.263 --> 00:02:17.488
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:21.306
5 and 6.

42
00:02:21.306 --> 00:02:26.608
Record 5 again, now on its second delivery, and on a different reader.

43
00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:29.032
And 6 started before 5 finished.

44
00:02:29.032 --> 00:02:30.092
Order is gone.

45
00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:31.298
Now c1 freezes.

46
00:02:31.298 --> 00:02:35.836
The Java runtime stops it to clean up memory, and it says nothing.

47
00:02:35.836 --> 00:02:39.756
You might think the group has to notice c1 is gone first.

48
00:02:39.756 --> 00:02:40.512
It doesn't.

49
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:48.317
At 10:00:30 the locks on 0 and 1 run out, and the broker makes them available again, for anyone.

50
00:02:48.317 --> 00:02:50.697
Nobody had to notice anything.

51
00:02:50.867 --> 00:02:54.246
When c1 wakes up and answers, its answers are refused.

52
00:02:54.246 --> 00:02:58.689
Like coming back from a nap to find your orders cooked by someone else.

53
00:02:58.689 --> 00:03:03.132
So the point is: a lock that runs out frees the work without a meeting.

54
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.901
c3 picks up 0 and 1, their second delivery, and accepts both.

55
00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:12.124
Now 0 to 4 are all done, so the start offset moves to 5.

56
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.512
The share group forgets what's before it, but the records stay in the log for any other reader.

57
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.846
Record 5 keeps timing out.

58
00:03:19.846 --> 00:03:22.571
Released, taken, released, taken.

59
00:03:22.571 --> 00:03:24.717
Its count climbs: 3, 4, 5.

60
00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:29.174
On its fifth delivery, a release no longer makes it available.

61
00:03:29.174 --> 00:03:35.121
The limit is five by default, so the broker archives it: given up on, never delivered again.

62
00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:43.218
Since Kafka 4.4, the broker can also copy it to a dead-letter topic, with a header that says it was offset 5.

63
00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:47.485
Then 6 and 7 are accepted, and the start offset moves to 8.

64
00:03:47.633 --> 00:03:48.759
Here's the price.

65
00:03:48.759 --> 00:03:52.930
Readers work side by side, and released records come back late.

66
00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:57.168
So a share group keeps no order, not per key, not per partition.

67
00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.320
For text messages, that's fine.

68
00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.904
Two receipts can go out either way round.

69
00:04:01.904 --> 00:04:05.685
For a ledger, where a refund must follow its payment, never.

70
00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:13.608
And the broker keeps the knobs in bounds: the lock between 15 and 60 seconds, the delivery limit between 2 and 10.

71
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:18.342
Here's a reader that answers for every record, in Java and in Kotlin.

72
00:04:18.342 --> 00:04:22.785
It reads with KafkaShareConsumer, which has the same poll as before.

73
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.009
Sent: ACCEPT.

74
00:04:24.009 --> 00:04:25.483
Timed out: RELEASE.

75
00:04:25.483 --> 00:04:27.579
A bad phone number: REJECT.

76
00:04:27.579 --> 00:04:31.304
Then commitSync sends the answers to the broker.

77
00:04:31.833 --> 00:04:35.143
So, what stops two cooks from making the same order?

78
00:04:35.143 --> 00:04:36.479
A lock on every slip.

79
00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:43.416
Any free reader takes any record, the lock runs out by itself, and the count stops a record that never works.

80
00:04:43.633 --> 00:04:44.758
One quick check.

81
00:04:44.758 --> 00:04:48.341
A reader takes record 9 and then crashes, silently.

82
00:04:48.341 --> 00:04:50.941
When can another reader get record 9?

83
00:04:50.941 --> 00:04:52.065
Pause and think.

84
00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.345
When its 30-second lock runs out.

85
00:04:57.345 --> 00:05:02.055
Then it's available again, and the next reader to take it makes its count 2.
